TI  - Land use land cover transformation and seasonal variations in land surface temperature with environmental implications in Gazipur Sadar Upazila, Bangladesh
PY  - 2025
T2  - Urban Climate
VL  - 64
C7  - 102662
DO  - 10.1016/j.uclim.2025.102662
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105020028930&doi=10.1016%2Fj.uclim.2025.102662&partnerID=40&md5=f21331db1968644781097847168a2d8b
AB  - Rapid urbanization in Gazipur Sadar Upazila, Bangladesh, has significantly transformed land use land cover (LULC), contributing to rising land surface temperature (LST), and the intensification of urban heat island (UHI) effects. This study investigated the spatiotemporal changes in land use and land cover (LULC) and land surface temperature (LST) from 1999 to 2024. The changes in LST through different LULC classes were examined, and the correlation between various environmental indices and LST were assessed. This study also explored the spatial patterns and intensities of the urban heat island (UHI) effect and the urban thermal field variance index (UTFVI). Cloud-free (<5 %) Landsat 5 TM and Landsat 9 OLI/TIRS images from 1999, 2011, and 2024 (winter and summer seasons) were acquired from the USGS Earth Explorer and used to classify LULC (urban area, vegetation, waterbody, and bare land) and derive the LST. Supervised classification revealed that urban areas expanded by 93 % (from 61.3 km2 to 118.3 km2), reducing vegetation by 9 % and water bodies by 45 %. Urban growth at an average rate of 2.7 % from 1999 to 2024, emphasized accelerated land conversion. Summer LST increased by 5.7 °C (from 26.8 °C in 1999 to 32.5 °C in 2024), with urban and bare land showing the highest temperatures. A strong positive correlation was observed between the LST and the normalized difference built-up index (NDBI), whereas with the normalized difference vegetation index (NDVI), the correlation was weakly negative. The urban thermal field variance index (UTFVI) revealed worsening ecological conditions in densely urbanized zones. The findings suggest the need for green infrastructure, water body conservation, and sustainable land-use policies to reduce heat stress and protect the environment. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
