TI  - Assessing compound exposure of vulnerable populations under dual inequality of urban heatwaves and green space
PY  - 2025
T2  - Sustainable Cities and Society
VL  - 134
C7  - 106951
DO  - 10.1016/j.scs.2025.106951
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105020374853&doi=10.1016%2Fj.scs.2025.106951&partnerID=40&md5=88aeabf684bd06e86d37ba5d1e8c491a
AB  - Urban populations are increasingly exposed to climate-induced health risks, with extreme heat and insufficient green space emerging as key environmental stressors. However, limited research has examined the compound effects of exposure to urban heatwaves and green space scarcity, especially its impacts on vulnerable groups such as the elderly and children. This study integrates long-term datasets on land surface temperature, vegetation coverage, and population structure to assess the spatiotemporal dynamics of urban heatwaves and green spaces in 657 cities across China from 2003 to 2022. We developed a Composite Environmental Exposure Index (CEI) to quantify compound exposure levels and evaluate disparities among vulnerable populations. The results show that urban heatwave characteristics have intensified significantly, with increasing frequency and stronger intensity—particularly in large cities—and a nearly 40-day extension of the heatwave season due to earlier onset and delayed termination. Green space distribution varies greatly, exhibiting an “east-high, west-low” pattern and higher coverage in smaller cities. The CEI has risen markedly, although inter-city inequality has narrowed over time. In contrast, exposure among vulnerable populations has continued to increase, with inequality remaining at a high level. Older adults and children have seen sustained CEI growth, with the elderly—especially women—accounting for 65–70 % of the total exposure. High-exposure zones are concentrated in urban agglomeration cores and megacities, with new hotspots emerging in central and western regions. By bridging the gap between compound environmental exposure and population vulnerability, this study provides critical insights for advancing equitable urban climate adaptation strategies. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
