TI  - Evaluating urban bioclimatic comfort in a metropolitan city Kolkata with urban dynamics and heat Islands
PY  - 2025
T2  - Advances in Space Research
VL  - 76
IS  - 6
SP  - 3372
EP  - 3388
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2025.07.012
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105011097430&doi=10.1016%2Fj.asr.2025.07.012&partnerID=40&md5=ffbbfb46cf9b780d141693f928979b57
AB  - The physical structure of cities is expanding at an unprecedented rate, particularly in metro centers. The altered physical environment, as well as profound anthropogenic activities in cities, has an impact on the urban environment, climate and human health. This study examines two decades of urban sprawl (1999–2020) in the Kolkata Metropolitan area (KMA) using high-resolution satellite and further to analyse bioclimatic comfort zones. The research focuses on evaluating Land Surface Temperature (LST), Urban Heat Island (UHI) intensity, and Urban Thermal Comfort Levels through the Robba Index and Heat Index. The results indicate that built-up areas expanded significantly, covering 42.11 % of the total study area in 2023, from 12.74 % in 1993. Conversely, vegetation cover reduced from 825.31 km2 in 1993 to 604.18 km2 in 2023. The increase in built-up areas has led to a rise in LST by 0.57 °C and has exacerbated UHI effects, resulting in greater thermal discomfort, particularly in April. Bioclimatic comfort zones based on the Robba Index analysis for April show that approximately 1,219.38 km2 (67 %) of the area experiences “Heat Stress” and 498.82 km2 (27.5 %) falls under “Extreme Heat”. May, however, displays a slight improvement with the “Comfortable” zone covering 320.65 km2 (17.64 %), owing to increased humidity and wind speed associated with pre-monsoon conditions. The findings highlight that the northern regions of Kolkata experience the highest levels of thermal discomfort due to dense urbanization and loss of greenery. Sustainable urban planning practices, such as increasing green cover, preserving natural landscapes and water bodies, are crucial to reducing UHI effects and improving bioclimatic comfort. Future research should explore green infrastructure solutions to mitigate UHI effects, enhance thermal comfort, and ensure a healthier urban environment for the rapidly growing KMA. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
