UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105011209347&doi=10.1016%2Fj.scs.2025.106597&partnerID=40&md5=eb3eb17502aa050b1d396825768f0031
AB  - This study examines how urban form and green space structure influence urban heat mitigation, focusing specifically on land surface temperature (LST). Urban form variables—including Density, Concentration, Clustering, and Continuity—were constructed to capture building-driven heat dynamics, while green space structure was characterized by landscape metrics (Area, Shape, Aggregation) derived from Fragstats. Using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modelling (PLS-SEM), this research identified direct and indirect relationships among these variables and their combined impact on LST. Results show that urban density alone does not directly increase temperatures; however, lower spatial concentration indirectly reduces green space effectiveness, raising LST. Conversely, compact urban development with higher spatial concentration significantly promotes heat mitigation by supporting larger, better-connected green areas. These findings emphasize the importance of spatially integrated green infrastructure in urban planning for effective thermal resilience. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
