TI  - Cooling efficacy assessment of urban green spaces using machine learning-based multivariate landscape analysis
PY  - 2025
T2  - Sustainable Cities and Society
VL  - 130
C7  - 106541
DO  - 10.1016/j.scs.2025.106541
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105009215570&doi=10.1016%2Fj.scs.2025.106541&partnerID=40&md5=2b85ac6bd74684a55342b8489ffbc51c
AB  - Urban green spaces (UGS) serve as a cost-effective Nature-based solution to combat extreme heat. This study investigates the role of UGS in cooling their surroundings during harsh summers in a hot-dry Indian city. The research is abetted with remote sensing, geostatistical modelling, and machine learning techniques of Random Forest and Multivariate Adaptive Regression Splines. It reveals that besides UGS size, their internal structure is also pivotal in modifying the thermal regime. On an average, 181 UGS are 2.9 °C cooler than the citywide mean land surface temperature of 34.07 °C. However, nearly 40 % UGS are smaller than the minimum area recommended by the national urban guidelines. Their uneven distribution further restricts their potential to cool uniformly. Buffer analysis determines a maximum cooling intensity of 4 °C, spanning a mean cooling distance of 30 m, and the threshold value of efficiency (TVoE) recommends an optimal UGS size of 3.0 ± 0.15 ha for this city. A comprehensive multivariate analysis identified nine key parameters including cooling indices (efficiency and gradient), landscape metrics (PARA, CONTIG, NCORE, PLAND, ED, and LSI) and surrounding vegetation cover. This parsimonious model elucidates over 50 % of UGS cooling performance and provides actionable insights to policymakers and urban planners for enhancing existing green spaces. The findings urge the revision of national urban guidelines by highlighting the pertinence of strategic landscape planning for augmented heat mitigation. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
