TI  - Modelling and optimization of urban green-blue infrastructure design for city cooling
PY  - 2025
T2  - Building and Environment
VL  - 279
C7  - 113096
DO  - 10.1016/j.buildenv.2025.113096
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105004259877&doi=10.1016%2Fj.buildenv.2025.113096&partnerID=40&md5=6c9d0c2b5ac49365e9f7817647c7fb84
AB  - Urban overheating is a growing concern under the dual challenges of global warming and urban heat island. A promising solution is the implementation of green-blue infrastructure (GBI). This study developed a new urban GBI model by coupling a new urban waterbody module with a state-of-the-art urban vegetation module in an urban canopy model. This coupling model captured realistic heat and moisture transfer processes over different GBI configurations and resolved complicated radiative exchanges between green (trees) and blue (waterbodies) infrastructures. Based on this model, we explored the synergistic cooling effect of various GBI designs in different urban forms, focusing on variables such as tree cover size, waterbody size, and waterbody-tree distance. Our findings indicated that increasing GBI size from 0 to 20 m provided significant synergistic cooling effect with canyon average temperature reductions of 2.5–4.8 °C, and humidity increase of 0.5–3.5 g/kg. This led to an obvious reduction in heatstroke risk, with daytime and nighttime decreases of 7.1–38.5 % and 7.7–20 % in heatstroke hours, respectively. However, excessive GBI sizes may counteract cooling benefits and heighten heat risk due to high humidity levels. Adjusting the waterbody-tree distance from 0 to 5 m can further enhance cooling, reducing the required GBI size by 0.3–4.2 m while maintaining similar benefits. This research emphasizes the significance of optimized GBI designs, suggesting that high-density urban blocks necessitate larger waterbodies, smaller tree canopies, and less waterbody-tree distances to create more effective cooling spots for urban residents facing aggravating heat risk. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
