TI  - Decoding the cooling potential of urban green spaces: A cross-city investigation of driving factors in 311 Chinese cities under varying climate zones
PY  - 2025
T2  - Sustainable Cities and Society
VL  - 126
C7  - 106410
DO  - 10.1016/j.scs.2025.106410
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105004192261&doi=10.1016%2Fj.scs.2025.106410&partnerID=40&md5=b99e75e9690524b530f6ad79aab22d03
AB  - Urban green space (UGS), as a key strategy for mitigating the urban heat island effect, has its cooling effect influenced by multiple factors. Although previous studies have explored the impact of landscape indicators of UGS on the cooling effect, the results have often been inconsistent and uncertain, likely due to differences in urban climate backgrounds and internal environments. To address these discrepancies, this study examines 311 prefecture-level cities in China, aiming to resolve conflicts through large-scale, cross-city research and answer three questions: (1) How does the cooling effect of UGS vary across cities with different climatic backgrounds? (2) What is the impact of landscape indicators on the cooling effect? (3) What is the optimal TVoE for cooling effect of UGS in each city? Through spatial analysis and multi-level modeling, we found that cooling effects vary systematically with climate zones: subtropical monsoon cities exhibited highest cooling intensity, while temperate regions showed greater cooling distance. The cooling intensity was primarily determined by the interplay between landscape composition and configuration. Further analysis revealed that the TVoE for optimal cooling benefits varied significantly across cities, strongly correlating with urban temperature and humidity. These findings provide quantitative guidelines for climate-adaptive urban greening strategies. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
