TI  - Dual potential of Garden Rooftop Farms for heat island mitigation and spatial productivity
PY  - 2025
T2  - Building and Environment
VL  - 274
C7  - 112768
DO  - 10.1016/j.buildenv.2025.112768
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85218895943&doi=10.1016%2Fj.buildenv.2025.112768&partnerID=40&md5=3960a56b9ba5a0f4f1b73be1f86c7eaa
AB  - Garden rooftop farms (GRFs), as an emerging form of urban green infrastructure, offer significant benefits for climate regulation and the revitalization of public spaces. This study explores the development potential of GRFs by conducting detailed microclimate measurements during a high-temperature summer period. This study (i) established a framework for evaluating GRF development potential, focusing on urban heat island (UHI) mitigation and urban spatial productivity (USP) levels, (ii) calculated and analyzed the UHI mitigation potential and underlying mechanism under typical summer weather conditions, and (iii) evaluated USP levels across 208 GRF development scenarios, ranked from Level-I (highest) to Level-V (lowest). The results indicate that GRFs had notable UHI mitigation potential, with a maximum daily average reduction of approximately 1.25 °C during typical summer months. Compared to bare roofs (BR), GRFs exhibited higher radiative income and a lower Bowen ratio energy balance pattern. The high evapotranspiration and low heat storage characteristics of GRFs were the primary drivers of UHI mitigation. Overall, USP levels declined across four weather types (from overcast to clear), with the proportion of development scenarios at Level-II or above dropping from 95.23 % to 14.29 %. However, certain scenarios, such as those involving morning exercise or evening dining, showed minimal sensitivity to weather conditions, maintaining Level-II or higher USP proportions of 75.00 % and 100.00 %, respectively, across all weather types. These scenarios were highly recommended for GRF operations during summer. Additionally, temporal distribution and thermal stress redundancy analyses suggested that adjusting functional activity schedules and implementing GRFs during cooler seasons could further enhance USP levels. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
