TI  - Exploring the relationship of land surface parameters and air pollutants with land surface temperature in different cities using satellite data
PY  - 2024
T2  - Advances in Space Research
VL  - 74
IS  - 7
SP  - 2958
EP  - 2975
DO  - 10.1016/j.asr.2024.06.031
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85196961883&doi=10.1016%2Fj.asr.2024.06.031&partnerID=40&md5=00169a9b8550b9981415fa4a3d1a2599
AB  - Most cities around the world are experiencing rapid expansion and urbanization as a result of increasing industrial activity and construction while reducing green space availability which impacts our thermal environment. The variation of both the land cover distribution and the air pollutant concentration impacts the LST pattern in different cities. Thus, the present study investigates the relation of Land Surface temperature (LST) with each land cover parameters such as Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI), Normalized Difference Built-up Index (NDBI), Apparent Thermal Inertia (ATI), artificial sensible heat flux (Has) and air pollutants such as Carbon monoxide (CO), Nitrogen dioxide (NO<inf>2</inf>), Sulphur Dioxide (SO<inf>2</inf>), ozone (O<inf>3</inf>)) in four different cities in India using satellite data. The correlation of LST with NDVI and ATI were found negative whereas with NDBI and Has were found positive in the four cities. The impact of each air pollutant on LST was found to vary with cities. Further, the combined influence of all the parameters on LST was analysed using ANOVA test and percentage contribution of each parameter towards LST was determined. The combined percentage contribution of land surface parameters such as NDVI, NDBI and ATI were found to be 97.8, 98.0, 74.4 and 84.4 for Bangalore, Bikaner, Ahmedabad and Varanasi, respectively. The combined percentage contribution of air pollutants such as CO, NO<inf>2</inf>, SO<inf>2</inf> and O<inf>3</inf> was found to be 1.3, 1.9, 2.9 and 12.9 for Bangalore, Bikaner, Ahmedabad and Varanasi, respectively. Therefore, the present study reveals that the land cover distribution had greater influence on LST than air pollutants in different cities. © 2024 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
